\title{OPT-G: transparent huge pages for the heap of the executable - 2 to 14 percent of a pass while the kernel has them, a loss of 10 to 50 percent when it has none; in the image, off by default}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-25}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{platform}
\tag{gc}
\tag{performance}
\tag{benchmark}
\tag{arena}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Agent "OPT-G" of the second optimisation wave after the first release, 2026-11-25 (11:56 to 17:00 UTC). The lever was found an hour earlier by the agent of the loader's second pass: the JVM launcher runs with transparent huge pages and the native image, which is [the release](design-packaging), has none. This report is what was tried to give the one executable huge pages for its heap, the mechanism that works, what it is worth and what it can cost under [the guard for a change of the default configuration](dec-YP0N), and what it does to small inputs, to memory and under each system setting. Everything was done in private copies of the tracked sources of the main tree; nothing was published. The result is one patch (section 14) and a verdict against making the advice a default (section 0).}

\subtree{
\title{0. Answer}

\ol{
\li{\strong{The mechanism that works is a wrapper around the image's \code{mmap}, linked into the executable.} GraalVM CE 25 has no option for transparent huge pages. Advice given once to the image's reserved address range is lost, because the serial collector commits every aligned chunk and every large array with an \code{mmap} of its own and each is a new mapping (measured: 0 MiB of huge pages in a Mathlib pass). The linker option \code{\verb~|--wrap=mmap~} with one C object lets the executable advise each mapping after it is made; it is still one file with the same shared-library dependencies (sections 3 and 4).}
\li{\strong{Where huge pages can be had they are worth 2 to 14 \% of a warm pass.} The same executable with and without the advice, 8 workers, three interleaved rounds, the other cores of the host busy: Mathlib 14.58 to 14.23 s (-2.4 \%), cslib 5.42 to 5.18 s (-4.4 \%), cedar -3.3 \%, Std -3.9 \%, con-leche -6.9 \%, reachability 0.522 to 0.448 s (-14.1 \%); the loader thread needs 12 to 13 \% less CPU on Mathlib and cslib, the workers 2 to 4 \%. The arena's 18 memory-heavy \code{perf} tests are 9 to 40 \% faster, a cold Mathlib run in the arena-shaped container takes 14.7 instead of 16.2 s. 60 to 65 \% of the resident set is then in huge pages; the resident set, the container's memory account and the arena's instruction count do not change (sections 5, 8, 9, 11).}
\li{\strong{Where the kernel has no huge page to give, the same advice costs 10 to 50 \%.} With the system settings of Ubuntu (\code{enabled=madvise}, \code{defrag=madvise}) a first touch in an advised mapping does not fall back to a small page: it waits for the kernel to compact memory. In five minutes of the guard window navier-stokes-euler was 11.6 \% and Init 8.8 \% slower with the advice than the same executable without; twenty minutes later, when the host's 23 GB of free memory held no block of 2 MiB, a cslib pass took 7.7 to 9.5 s instead of 5.7 to 6.5 s and a Mathlib pass 15.6 to 19.1 s instead of 14.4 to 16.8 s, while the host counted 11,060 allocations entering compaction during one cslib process (1,800 to 2,200 during a process without advice), 11,058 of them in vain (section 6). The JVM launcher's \code{\verb~|-XX:+UseTransparentHugePages~} has the same exposure.}
\li{\strong{Verdict for the default configuration: NO-GO for advice by default.} The guard of [the rule for a default](dec-YP0N) is not met by the first candidate (plain advice, on by default): the geometric mean of the eight warm passes goes down by 2.1 \%, but navier-stokes-euler is 15.7 \% and Init 8.0 \% slower than the base, where 3 \% is the limit (section 5). Whether a machine has free 2 MiB blocks is not in the executable's hands, and the arena's machine, 16 GB with a 6.3 GB export in its page cache, is of the kind that may have few.}
\li{\strong{What is delivered instead: the wrapper in the image, switched off, and watching when it is switched on} (section 7). A plain run gives no advice and makes the system calls of the base. With \code{\verb~|RLVM_HUGEPAGES=on~} the wrapper advises a mapping only if \code{\verb~|/proc/buddyinfo~} shows the kernel has that much and 256 MiB more free in blocks of 2 MiB, and it takes its advice back from everything when those blocks run out or \code{\verb~|/proc/vmstat~} shows allocations entering compaction. Its logic was tested with made-up kernel files, and on the host as it was it did what it is for, in counts: with free blocks it advised 57 to 63 mappings of a reachability run and the process spent 0.09 to 0.17 s in the kernel instead of 0.29 to 0.33 s; with none it advised nothing and the kernel time was that of the run without advice (12 and 16 pairs of runs). \code{\verb~|rlvm --version~} names the setting in its \code{\verb~|build:~} line. Making the watched advice the default is one word of the build script and a decision that needs its own guard, taken in both states of a machine's memory; this report does not have that guard (item 7).}
\li{\strong{Safety.} No Java source, class file or jar changes; the jars of the final candidate are byte for byte the shared distribution's with the level-store repair of this afternoon. The gate of the default configuration through the final executable has the counts of the release (230 of 230 tests; 17,775 mutants with SOUNDNESS 0 and COMPLETENESS 0, agree 15,741, declined 2,034, no error; 1,196 hostile rows with no wrong accept and no wrong reject), the eight corpora are accepted with 8 and 4 workers through both launchers, and every counter of the eight corpora is identical to the base's. A tutorial test is 21 MB and 0.01 to 0.02 s as before: nothing below 2 MiB is ever advised, and 241 of the arena's 262 small tests never make such a mapping (sections 9 and 12).}
\li{\strong{Not measured}: a quiet host; x86-64; the system settings \code{never} and \code{always} (\code{never} approximated with a process for which huge pages are disabled: no effect, no error); a machine short of memory, such as the arena's; the watched advice under a stall in a timing window, and with it a guard for it as a default; the optional Oracle GraalVM build, which gets the same treatment and was not built (section 13).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. The question}

\p{The profile of the loader thread by the agent of the loader's second pass ("OPT-F", 2026-11-25) ended in a finding outside its modules: every memory-bound item of the loader thread stalls on one load with a walk of the page tables, 760,000 of the 867,000 page faults of a Mathlib pass are first touches of 4 KiB pages of the heap, and the JVM launcher has transparent huge pages (\code{\verb~|-XX:+UseTransparentHugePages~}, worth 6 \% of a warm Mathlib pass there, see [the JVM as the warm platform](rep-J1) and [the launcher line](dec-E3EX)) while the native image has none. Its experiment was a preloaded library of 30 lines on the unchanged executable that advises every anonymous writable mapping of 2 MiB or more: cslib 4,997 to 4,797 ms and Mathlib 14.28 to 13.76 s over two rounds on a host that was not quiet. A preloaded library is a second file, and [the release is one executable](design-packaging). The task of this report: give the executable itself huge pages for its heap, by whatever mechanism an experiment shows to work, and measure what that is worth under [the guard for a change of the default configuration](dec-YP0N).}

\p{\strong{Host.} aarch64, 10 Cortex-X925 and 10 Cortex-A725, 121 GB, Linux 7.0, base page 4 KiB, huge page 2 MiB. \code{\verb~|/sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/enabled~} is \code{madvise} and \code{defrag} is \code{madvise}, which are Ubuntu's defaults; the smaller sizes of multi-size huge pages (16 KiB to 1 MiB) are all \code{never}; \code{khugepaged} scans 4,096 pages every 10 s. The settings are the host's and could not be changed (no privilege), also not for a container.}
}

\subtree{
\title{2. How the image gets its memory}

\p{The \code{mmap} calls of the unchanged executable were logged with an experiment library (the calls the C library makes for itself, thread stacks and \code{malloc}, are not among them).}

\ul{
\li{\strong{One reservation.} At start the image reserves 32 GiB + 512 KiB of address space with one \code{\verb~|mmap(PROT_NONE, MAP_NORESERVE)~} and maps its image heap, 26.7 MB, from the executable file into it. Everything the collector allocates later lies inside this range.}
\li{\strong{Every commit is a mapping of its own.} The serial collector commits memory with \code{\verb~|mmap(addr, n, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE, MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANONYMOUS | MAP_FIXED)~} over a part of the reservation: one call for each aligned heap chunk of 512 KiB (the default of GraalVM CE 25.5.5; small objects live in these) and one call for each array of 128 KiB or more, which is a chunk of its own (\code{\verb~|-H:LargeArrayThreshold=131072~}). It gives memory back with the same call and \code{\verb~|PROT_NONE~}. A large array is never copied by a collection; its chunk is unmapped when the array is garbage.}
\li{\strong{A tutorial test} (\code{\verb~|tutorial/001_basicDef~}, 21 MB resident, 0.01 to 0.02 s): 18 calls; 16 commits of 6.2 MiB together, the largest 1 MiB + 4 KiB.}
\li{\strong{One Mathlib pass} (8 workers): 5,162 commits of 5,721 MiB together and 1,602 uncommits. By size:}
}

\table{
\tr{\th{Size of the mapping}\th{Mappings}\th{MiB}\th{Share of the committed bytes}}
\tr{\td{128 KiB to below 512 KiB (arrays)}\td{3,212}\td{783}\td{13.7 \%}}
\tr{\td{512 KiB exactly (aligned chunks)}\td{952}\td{476}\td{8.3 \%}}
\tr{\td{above 512 KiB to below 1 MiB}\td{236}\td{138}\td{2.4 \%}}
\tr{\td{1 MiB to below 2 MiB}\td{495}\td{520}\td{9.1 \%}}
\tr{\td{\strong{below 2 MiB, together}}\td{\strong{4,896}}\td{\strong{1,917}}\td{\strong{33.5 \%}}}
\tr{\td{2 MiB to below 4 MiB}\td{138}\td{313}\td{5.5 \%}}
\tr{\td{4 MiB to below 16 MiB}\td{86}\td{510}\td{8.9 \%}}
\tr{\td{16 MiB to below 128 MiB}\td{40}\td{1,205}\td{21.1 \%}}
\tr{\td{432 MiB (one) and 1,343 MiB (one)}\td{2}\td{1,775}\td{31.0 \%}}
\tr{\td{\strong{2 MiB or more, together}}\td{\strong{266}}\td{\strong{3,804}}\td{\strong{66.5 \%}}}
}

\p{So two thirds of what a Mathlib pass commits is in 266 mappings that are each large enough to hold huge pages, and a small input never makes such a mapping.}
}

\subtree{
\title{3. Three mechanisms, by experiment}

\subtree{
\title{3.1 An option of the image: there is none}

\p{\code{\verb~|native-image --expert-options-all~} of GraalVM CE 25.5.5.0.3 (2,359 lines) has no option that names huge pages, large pages or \code{madvise}, neither among the build options (\code{\verb~|-H:~}) nor among the run-time ones (\code{\verb~|-R:~}). What it has near the subject: \code{\verb~|-H:AlignedHeapChunkSize~} (524,288), \code{\verb~|-H:LargeArrayThreshold~} (131,072), \code{\verb~|-H:+AlwaysPreTouch~} (touches committed pages, does not change their size), \code{\verb~|-H:ReservedAddressSpaceSize~}, and \code{\verb~|-H:NativeLinkerOption~}, which passes a word to the linker of the image and is what section 3.3 uses.}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.2 Advice for the reserved range at start-up: lost at the first commit}

\p{The image commits inside one reservation, so one call at start-up that advises the whole range would be the smallest change. It does not work, because of how the commits are made:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{In a C program of 60 lines} that does what the image does: a reservation of 1 GiB advised with \code{\verb~|MADV_HUGEPAGE~} carries the flag (\code{hg} in \code{\verb~|/proc/self/smaps~}); a commit by \code{\verb~|mmap(MAP_FIXED)~} of 64 MiB inside it is a new mapping without the flag, and touching all of it gives 0 kB of \code{AnonHugePages}; the same commit advised after the \code{mmap} and before the first touch gives 65,536 kB; an uncommit and a second commit of the same range lose the flag again. (A commit by \code{mprotect} would keep the flag; the image does not commit that way.)}
\li{\strong{On the executable}, with the experiment library advising the reservation and nothing else: one Mathlib pass, peak resident set 4,009 MiB, peak \code{AnonHugePages} 0 MiB, 1.46 million page faults, as without any advice (3,866 MiB, 0 MiB, 1.48 million). \code{\verb~|/proc/PID/smaps~} 40 s into such a pass: the 292 writable mappings inside the reservation (3,806 MiB, 3,026 MiB resident) carry no flag and have no huge page; the flag is left on the 28.7 GiB that were never committed, and the ranges that were committed and given back (189 mappings, 184 MiB) have lost it as well.}
}

\p{The advice has to be given to each mapping after it is made, so it has to sit where the mappings are made.}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.3 A wrapper around the image's \code{mmap}, linked in: works}

\p{The image calls the C library's \code{mmap} through an ordinary undefined symbol of its object file. The linker option \code{\verb~|--wrap=mmap~} (GNU ld, gold, lld and mold have it) resolves every such reference in the objects it links to \code{\verb~|__wrap_mmap~} and gives the wrapper the real function as \code{\verb~|__real_mmap~}. So one C object with a wrapper of that name, handed to the linker of the image through \code{\verb~|-H:NativeLinkerOption~}, sees every mapping the image's own code makes, and no mapping the C library makes for itself (thread stacks, \code{malloc}): those calls are inside the shared library and are not relinked. The executable stays one file with the same shared-library dependencies; its table of dynamic symbols is the base's, line for line (272 entries).}

\p{The wrapper calls \code{mmap} and, when the new mapping is anonymous, private, writable and at least 2 MiB long, calls \code{\verb~|madvise(MADV_HUGEPAGE)~} on it. It touches no page. Measured first with the experiment library on the unchanged executable, one Mathlib pass each, not timings:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Advice}\th{Mappings advised}\th{MiB advised}\th{Peak resident set, MiB}\th{Peak \code{AnonHugePages}, MiB}\th{Page faults of the process}\th{Kernel time, s}}
\tr{\td{none}\td{0}\td{0}\td{3,866}\td{0}\td{1,479,507}\td{3.2}}
\tr{\td{the reservation only (section 3.2)}\td{1}\td{-}\td{4,009}\td{0}\td{1,461,289}\td{3.0}}
\tr{\td{every writable mapping of 2 MiB or more}\td{257}\td{3,749}\td{3,957}\td{2,460}\td{663,874}\td{1.7}}
\tr{\td{every writable mapping of 128 KiB or more}\td{4,924}\td{5,677}\td{3,775}\td{2,366}\td{672,036}\td{1.9}}
}

\ul{
\li{With the advice 62 \% of the resident set at its peak is in huge pages and the process takes 0.66 instead of 1.48 million page faults. \code{\verb~|/proc/PID/smaps~} of the candidate executable of section 4, 40 s into a Mathlib pass: 81 writable mappings carry the flag (2,787 MiB, 2,361 MiB resident, 2,166 MiB of that in huge pages) and 399 do not (1,045 MiB, no huge page).}
\li{\strong{Advising the smaller mappings as well gives nothing}: not one more huge page. A mapping below 2 MiB cannot hold a huge page by itself; neighbours that are advised do merge into one mapping, but the first chunk of a 2 MiB range is touched (its header is written) before its neighbours are committed, and a range that already has small pages gets a huge page only from \code{khugepaged}, which scans 16 MiB in 10 s here. So the threshold is one huge page, and the 1.9 GiB of a Mathlib pass that are committed in pieces below 2 MiB stay on small pages (section 8.2).}
\li{A large array does not start on a 2 MiB boundary (the collector places it by first fit), so its first and last partial huge page stay small: an array of 8 MiB gets three huge pages, 6 MiB, measured in a test image.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.4 Not built}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Replacing the image's memory provider} (a build-time feature that registers a subclass of Substrate VM's virtual-memory provider which advises after a commit). It needs no linker option, but it is written against internal classes of one GraalVM version, and the optional build uses another version.}
\li{\strong{Collapsing ranges synchronously} (\code{\verb~|MADV_COLLAPSE~}, Linux 6.1) for the ranges that are committed in small pieces: it copies what is there and makes untouched pages resident.}
\li{\strong{A preloaded library or a wrapper script}: a second file, excluded by the rule of one executable.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. The first candidate: plain advice}

\ul{
\li{\strong{One C source}, \src{rlvm-cli/src/main/native/hugepages.c}: \code{\verb~|__wrap_mmap~} as described in section 3.3. It is the only native source of the project. It lives in \code{rlvm-cli} because that module is the launcher and owns what the image is: the entry point, the image-wide \code{\verb~|native-image.properties~} and the \code{\verb~|build:~} line of \code{\verb~|rlvm --version~}. Maven does not see it; no jar changes.}
\li{\strong{The build} (\src{scripts/build.sh}) compiles it with the C compiler native-image itself uses (\code{gcc}, else \code{cc}), links a program of five lines with it and \code{\verb~|-Wl,--wrap=mmap~} and runs that program: only if it reports the advice for its 4 MiB mapping and none for its 1 MiB mapping does the image get \code{\verb~|-H:NativeLinkerOption=<the object> -H:NativeLinkerOption=-Wl,--wrap=mmap~}. If the compiler or the linker cannot do it, the image is built without the wrapper and the log says so; \code{\verb~|--no-hugepages~} builds without it on purpose.}
\li{\strong{The verification of the built image} has one more step: a run that fills a 128 MB heap with arrays of 8 MiB (\code{\verb~|--protocol-selftest oom-real~}) under \code{\verb~|RLVM_HUGEPAGES=trace~} must report advised mappings, 16 in the candidate. An image that was linked with the wrapper and does not go through it fails the build, so the text of \code{\verb~|--version~} cannot claim what the image does not do.}
\li{\strong{A switch for a run}: the environment variable \code{\verb~|RLVM_HUGEPAGES~}, read once at the first mapping of 2 MiB or more. It is in the environment and not an \code{\verb~|-X~} setting because the heap is committed below the launcher, before and independently of its command line. With \code{off} the executable makes exactly the system calls of the base, which gives a comparison \em{inside one executable}; section 5.3 says why that matters.}
\li{\strong{Where it does nothing by construction.} A mapping below 2 MiB, a mapping that is not anonymous, private and writable, a mapping with \code{\verb~|MAP_STACK~}; a base page other than 4 KiB (the huge page is then 32 MiB or 512 MiB, and what that does to the resident set was not measured). The wrapper keeps \code{errno}, ignores the result of \code{madvise}, uses no \code{stdio} and allocates nothing.}
\li{\strong{Trees and distributions.} "Base": a copy of the tracked sources of the main tree of 2026-11-25 11:56 UTC, built by \src{scripts/build.sh} with \code{\verb~|--published --dev~}; its 22 jars have the sha256 of the jars of the shared distribution of 09:13 UTC (jar set \code{\verb~|30dadd7f6cc5~}). "Candidate": the same copy with the patch, built the same way; the same 22 jars, so the JVM launcher of the two distributions is the same program, and the executable has the same table of dynamic symbols as the base's. Both executables are 68,356,216 bytes. Sections 5 and 6 are about this candidate; the final one is section 7.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. The guard of plain advice}

\subtree{
\title{5.1 How it was measured}

\p{The rule is [the guard for a change of the default configuration](dec-YP0N): the geometric mean of the warm passes of the eight corpora must go down and no corpus may be more than 3 \% slower, over at least three interleaved rounds. Every row was taken inside a quiet-core window (\src{bench/quiet.py}: the eight measurement cores belong to the window, one process at a time), three variants interleaved round by round in the order A B C, C B A, A B C: the base executable, the candidate executable, and the candidate executable with \code{\verb~|RLVM_HUGEPAGES=off~}. A run is the executable without options but \code{\verb~|--threads 8 --stats -X passes=N~}; a cell is the mean over the rounds of the median later pass of one process (no corpus was below the 0.5 s under which the rule takes the mean instead: reachability is 0.52 s on this busy host), with the smallest and the largest round in brackets. The other twelve cores of the host were busy with other agents' jobs the whole time (10 to 12 cores busy on average, load average 30 to 95), so the level of every number is higher than on a quiet host (Mathlib 13.1 s there on the same day, [the integration](rep-INT8)); the comparison is inside one window. Three windows: cslib at 12:22 UTC, Mathlib at 13:13, the other six corpora between 15:28 and 15:39.}
}

\subtree{
\title{5.2 The table}

\table{
\tr{\th{Corpus (rounds x passes)}\th{base, s}\th{candidate, s}\th{candidate without advice, s}\th{candidate against base}\th{candidate against itself without advice}}
\tr{\td{Init (3 x 10)}\td{0.668 (0.654 to 0.685)}\td{0.722 (0.653 to 0.858)}\td{0.663 (0.653 to 0.682)}\td{+8.0 \%}\td{+8.8 \%}}
\tr{\td{Std (3 x 10)}\td{1.138 (1.134 to 1.145)}\td{1.111 (1.110 to 1.112)}\td{1.156 (1.150 to 1.162)}\td{-2.4 \%}\td{-3.9 \%}}
\tr{\td{cedar (3 x 8)}\td{1.573 (1.547 to 1.590)}\td{1.507 (1.497 to 1.517)}\td{1.558 (1.541 to 1.581)}\td{-4.2 \%}\td{-3.3 \%}}
\tr{\td{reachability (3 x 15)}\td{0.519 (0.510 to 0.529)}\td{0.448 (0.437 to 0.458)}\td{0.522 (0.517 to 0.528)}\td{-13.6 \%}\td{-14.1 \%}}
\tr{\td{con-leche (3 x 6)}\td{1.515 (1.486 to 1.533)}\td{1.410 (1.382 to 1.445)}\td{1.514 (1.492 to 1.526)}\td{-6.9 \%}\td{-6.9 \%}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler (3 x 6)}\td{3.919 (3.850 to 3.963)}\td{4.534 (3.732 to 5.302)}\td{4.064 (3.966 to 4.125)}\td{+15.7 \%}\td{+11.6 \%}}
\tr{\td{cslib (3 x 6)}\td{5.490 (5.458 to 5.537)}\td{5.181 (5.109 to 5.316)}\td{5.420 (5.365 to 5.468)}\td{-5.6 \%}\td{-4.4 \%}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib (3 x 4)}\td{14.965 (14.870 to 15.116)}\td{14.229 (14.166 to 14.308)}\td{14.583 (14.345 to 14.740)}\td{-4.9 \%}\td{-2.4 \%}}
\tr{\td{\strong{geometric mean}}\td{\strong{2.042}}\td{\strong{1.999}}\td{\strong{2.043}}\td{\strong{-2.1 \%}}\td{\strong{-2.1 \%}}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The geometric mean goes down by 2.1 \%, and two corpora are far outside the guard}: navier-stokes-euler 15.7 \% and Init 8.0 \% slower than the base, 11.6 \% and 8.8 \% slower than the same executable without the advice. The guard is not met.}
\li{\strong{The other six corpora are 2.4 to 13.6 \% faster} than the base and 2.4 to 14.1 \% faster than the same executable without the advice: reachability 0.519 to 0.448 s, con-leche 1.515 to 1.410 s, cslib 5.490 to 5.181 s, cedar 1.573 to 1.507 s, Mathlib 14.97 to 14.23 s, Std 1.138 to 1.111 s.}
\li{The two losses and the six gains are the same advice in two states of the host's memory. navier-stokes-euler and Init were measured in the first five minutes of the third window, and in those five minutes the kernel had no huge page to give: section 6.}
}

\p{The first pass of each process, which is what one invocation of the executable costs (the pass's own wall clock, the mean of the three rounds):}

\table{
